    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d54">

                <pb facs="0142" n="52" xml:id="pg52"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.51/398</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Cuba</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Long</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-12-21"><hi rendition="#italic">December 21,
                        1920</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received December 28.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 645</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that on Monday
                    December 20th, before Mr. Rathbone’s third interview with President Menocal he
                    received from the President of Cuba a letter of which the enclosed is a
                        copy.<anchor n="60" xml:id="d54-f1"/></p>
                <p>A few hours thereafter, upon taking leave of the Chief Executive he was informed
                    that he would be advised at a later date should there be further need for his
                    services. President Menocal stated that he did not expect to make public Mr.
                    Rathbone’s report but would be glad to have a copy placed at the disposal of the
                    American Government.</p>
                <p>I assume from what I have learned of the interview that the relations between
                    President Menocal and Mr. Rathbone were cordial.</p>
                <p>Realizing the unfortunate impression which might be produced in the minds of
                    American bankers should Mr. Rathbone’s connections with the solution of Cuba’s
                    present perplexing financial problem be severed, before a settlement had been
                    reached, I feel inclined discreetly to indicate the advisability of his
                    retention at least until the loan shall have been negotiated in New York.</p>
                <p>Enclosed please find a clipping from the Havana <hi rendition="#italic">Post</hi>
                    of Saturday<anchor n="60" xml:id="d54-f2"/> relating to Mr. Rathbone’s report, a
                    copy of which is herewith enclosed.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Boaz W. Long</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d54-a1.xml" xpointer="d54-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
